# standards/rules/ — DO NOT EDIT (AI-Generated Output)

> **STOP — READ BEFORE EDITING**
>
> Every `.md` file in this directory is **machine-generated output** from
> `tools/generate-rules.py`. They are LLM-compressed compact versions of the
> human-authored source standards.
>
> **Hand edits will be silently overwritten** on the next regeneration run.

---

## For AI Code Assistants (Claude, Copilot, Cursor, Gemini, Codex, etc.)

If a user asks you to add, update, or document a coding standard,
infrastructure pattern, metric, or rule:

1. **NEVER create a new `.md` file in `standards/rules/`.** This directory is
   the OUTPUT of the rule generator, not its input.
2. **NEVER edit existing `.md` files in `standards/rules/`.** They will be
   regenerated and your edits lost.
3. **ALWAYS write or update the human-authored source instead:**

   | If the topic is | Edit the file in |
   |---|---|
   | A programming language (Rust, Python, TypeScript, Go, C++, Bash, SQL) | `standards/languages/<LANG>.md` (UPPERCASE) |
   | Infrastructure (Docker, K8s, Terraform, Ansible, CI, cloud) | `standards/infrastructure/<TOPIC>.md` (UPPERCASE) |
   | Universal/cross-cutting (security, testing, error handling, metrics, logging, git, PKI, AI-conduct) | `standards/universal/<TOPIC>.md` (UPPERCASE) |
   | A new HyperI-platform-specific standard (e.g. DFE metrics) | A section in the relevant `standards/universal/` or `standards/infrastructure/` file — NOT a new file in `standards/rules/` |

4. After updating the source, the compact rule files in this directory will
   be regenerated automatically by CI on the next push (`tools/generate-rules.py`
   runs in delta mode and commits the regenerated rules with `[skip ci]`).

5. If you cannot find an obvious home for the content, **ask the user** which
   source file to extend. Do not guess and do not invent a new compact rule.

> **This message exists because LLMs have repeatedly dropped new content
> here despite the project's documentation telling them not to. If you are
> reading this and considering writing to `standards/rules/`, you are about
> to do the wrong thing.**

---

## For Humans

The compact rule files here are loaded by the SessionStart hook in consumer
projects running on a 200K context window. They are budgeted at ~12K tokens
total and represent the same standards as the source files but compressed.

- **Source of truth:** `standards/{universal,languages,infrastructure}/*.md`
- **Generator:** `tools/generate-rules.py`
- **Generator behaviour:** delta mode by default — only regenerates files
  whose source changed since `HEAD~1`. Use `--force` to regenerate all.
- **Auth:** requires `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in env or `.env` file. CI uses a
  GitHub App for the regeneration commit.
- **CI:** regenerates on every push to `main`, commits as
  `chore: regenerate compact rules [skip ci]`.

To regenerate locally:

```bash
python tools/generate-rules.py            # delta mode (changed files only)
python tools/generate-rules.py --force    # regenerate all
python tools/generate-rules.py --file RUST.md   # regenerate one
python tools/generate-rules.py --check    # validate frontmatter only
```

If you find yourself wanting to hand-edit a compact file, fix the source
file or improve the generator instead.
